Deploy Applications from an Image and from a Template
Objectives
Identify the main resources and settings that Kubernetes uses to manage long-lived applications and demonstrate how OpenShift simplifies common application deployment workflows.

1. Deploying Applications in Kubernetes / OpenShift
Modern applications use microservices and containers.
•	Each application component runs in a container.
•	Kubernetes/OpenShift manages these containers using resources.
So instead of deploying a whole app at once, Kubernetes manages multiple resources like:
•	Pod
•	Deployment
•	Service
•	Secret
•	Persistent Volume
•	Template
•	Project
These resources together form the application.
________________________________________
2. Resources and Resource Definitions
A resource is a configuration object that tells Kubernetes what should run in the cluster.
Example resource types:
•	Pod
•	Deployment
•	Service
•	Secret
•	PersistentVolumeClaim
•	Template
Resources are usually written in YAML files.
Example:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
Important thing:
Creating a resource does not instantly create the object.
It sends a request to the cluster and Kubernetes eventually creates it.
________________________________________
3. Managing Resources
You can manage resources in:
•	YAML
•	JSON
Most common format → YAML
Example command:
Delete deployments using a label:
oc delete deployment -l app=my-app
Explanation:
Part	Meaning
oc	OpenShift CLI
delete	delete resource
deployment	resource type
-l app=my-app	select resources with label
Important concept:
Commands without namespace run in current namespace.
________________________________________
4. Templates (OpenShift Feature)
A Template is a YAML file that contains multiple resources with parameters.
Templates help deploy complex applications quickly.
Example resources inside template:
•	Secret
•	Service
•	Deployment
•	PersistentVolumeClaim
________________________________________
Processing a Template
Command:
oc process -f mysql-template.yaml -o yaml
Meaning:
Part	Meaning
oc process	process template
-f	template file
-o yaml	output YAML
Output will generate resources like:
•	Secret
•	Service
•	PVC
•	Deployment
________________________________________
View Template Parameters
Command:
oc process -f mysql-template.yaml --parameters
Shows configurable values like:
•	MYSQL_USER
•	MYSQL_PASSWORD
•	MYSQL_DATABASE
•	VOLUME_CAPACITY
________________________________________
Deploy Template using new-app
Command:
oc new-app --template mysql-persistent
This creates:
•	Secret
•	Service
•	PVC
•	Deployment
Example output resources:
secret "mysql" created
service "mysql" created
persistentvolumeclaim "mysql" created
deployment.apps "mysql" created
So one command deploys the whole application.
________________________________________
5. Pod
A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
A pod contains:
•	One or more containers
•	Shared networking
•	Shared storage
Example Pod YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: registry
spec:
  containers:
  - name: registry
    image: openshift/origin-docker-registry:v0.6.2
    ports:
    - containerPort: 5000
Important parts:
Section	Purpose
apiVersion	API version
kind	resource type
metadata	name, labels
spec	container details
status	current pod state
________________________________________
6. Deployment
A Deployment manages pods and ensures the desired number of replicas run.
Deployment provides:
•	Pod creation
•	Scaling
•	Rolling updates
•	Self-healing
Example YAML:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: hello-openshift
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: hello-openshift
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: hello-openshift
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: hello-openshift
        image: openshift/hello-openshift:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
Important fields:
Field	Meaning
replicas	number of pods
selector	identifies pods
template	pod definition
image	container image
________________________________________
7. Projects (OpenShift)
A Project is an enhanced Kubernetes Namespace.
Used for:
•	resource isolation
•	user access control
•	organizing applications
Example YAML:
apiVersion: project.openshift.io/v1
kind: Project
metadata:
  name: test
Projects use RBAC for permissions.
________________________________________
8. Services
Pods are temporary.
A Service provides stable networking access to pods.
Service works using labels and selectors.
Example YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: docker-registry
spec:
  selector:
    app: MyApp
  ports:
  - protocol: TCP
    port: 80
    targetPort: 9376
Explanation:
Field	Meaning
selector	finds pods with label
port	service port
targetPort	pod port
Service routes traffic → pods.
________________________________________
9. Persistent Volume Claims (PVC)
Containers normally lose data when they restart.
PVC allows persistent storage.
Process:
1.	Admin creates Persistent Volume (PV)
2.	Developer creates PVC
3.	Kubernetes binds PVC to PV
Example YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: mysql-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
  storageClassName: nfs-storage
Important fields:
Field	Meaning
accessModes	read/write permissions
storage	requested storage
storageClassName	storage type
________________________________________
10. Secrets
Secrets store sensitive data.
Examples:
•	passwords
•	API tokens
•	SSH keys
•	certificates
Example YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: example-secret
type: Opaque
data:
  username: bXl1c2Vy
  password: bXlQQDU1
Important:
•	data → base64 encoded
•	stringData → plain text
Secrets can be used:
•	as environment variables
•	as mounted files
